internlm3_8b_instruct__arc_test_00006_73fea8aa__temp0.10__seed7,internlm3_8b_instruct,internlm/InternLM3-8B-Instruct,arc_test_00006_73fea8aa,arc,arc_challenge,arc,6,A,1,"When considering which substance, when mixed with water, will produce the best conductor of electricity, it's important to think about the properties of each option. Table salt (sodium chloride) dissociates into ions in water, which can conduct electricity. Granulated sugar (sucrose) does not dissociate into ions and thus does not conduct electricity. Carbon dioxide is a gas and does not dissolve well in water to form a conductive solution. Motor oil is a hydrophobic substance and does not mix with water, making it an insulator. Therefore, table salt is the most likely to produce a good conductor of electricity when mixed with water.",A,A,1,90,0,0,104,256,-0.15328140556812286,0.47444114089012146,0.9539539813995361,16.346790313720703,0.0,0.0,0.0,3.46,1.0,101.91806556703523,2.511821614506797,16.430450916290283,7,0.1,cuda,minimal_json,default,0,1,json_exact,json_field,json_field,json_field,1,0,726,256,"```json
