internlm3_8b_instruct__arc_test_00290_cda60f09__temp0.10__seed7,internlm3_8b_instruct,internlm/InternLM3-8B-Instruct,arc_test_00290_cda60f09,arc,arc_challenge,arc,290,A,2,"Now that I understand the difference between mixtures and compounds, I need to evaluate each option to see which one fits the definition of a mixture. Oxygen and nitrogen in air are both gases that exist together without forming a new substance, so they are a mixture. Sodium and chlorine in salt form sodium chloride (NaCl), which is a compound. Hydrogen and oxygen in water form H2O, another compound. Nitrogen and hydrogen in ammonia form NH3, which is also a compound. Therefore, the correct answer is the one where the substances are not chemically bonded but remain as separate entities.",A,A,1,95,1,0,100,256,-0.1732315868139267,0.495569109916687,1.0866763591766357,16.631196975708008,8.677543640136719,0.09139460325241089,0.25274725274725274,3.51,0.95,81.45430730469525,3.1428663317015704,16.430450916290283,7,0.1,cuda,minimal_json,default,0,1,json_exact,json_field,json_field,json_field,1,0,677,256,"```json
{
