internlm3_8b_instruct__arc_test_00315_04264bf6__temp0.10__seed7,internlm3_8b_instruct,internlm/InternLM3-8B-Instruct,arc_test_00315_04264bf6,arc,arc_challenge,arc,315,D,1,"When considering the properties of waves, including Rossby waves, the relationship between wavelength, amplitude, and frequency is crucial. Wavelength (λ) is the distance between two consecutive crests or troughs, while frequency (f) is the number of waves passing a point per unit time. The speed of a wave (v) is the product of its wavelength and frequency (v = λ * f). If the wavelength is doubled, the speed of the wave remains constant, but the frequency must adjust to maintain this relationship. Therefore, if λ doubles, f must halve to keep v constant.",D,D,1,90,0,0,92,256,-0.1488349437713623,0.48427000641822815,0.9579739570617676,17.107837677001953,0.0,0.0,0.0,3.46,1.0,71.62316310685128,3.5742626951295833,16.430450916290283,7,0.1,cuda,minimal_json,default,0,1,json_exact,json_field,json_field,json_field,1,0,645,256,"```json
